# An expletive opener fills the subject slot with `there` or `it` and
# moves the real subject later (`There are three files that hold the
# config` becomes `Three files hold the config`, and `it is possible
# that the cache is stale` becomes `the cache might be stale`). A
# pseudo-cleft (`What the hook does is skip the file`) and `the reason
# is that` do the same.
#
# `there is` counts only with a relative pronoun one to three words
# later, since `there is a lock` states that a thing exists. Each gap
# length is its own alternative, the way the not-only rule writes its
# `but` tail. `it is advisable that` stays out, because the obligation
# rule reports `it is advisable`.
#
# It hit 63 lines in the kubernetes and nix.dev docs, and 9 of 10
# sampled hits rewrote with the real subject first. The miss took
# `that` as a determiner (`there's interest in that subject`).
expr there [(there is), (there are), (there was), (there were), there's]
expr rel [that, which, who]
expr does [does, do, did]
expr main [(@there * @rel), (@there * * @rel), (@there * * * @rel), ([(it is), (it was), it's] <ADJ, !advisable> that), (what * @does is), (what * * @does is), (what * * * @does is), (the reason [is, was] that)]
let message "Expletive opener. Make the real subject the subject of the sentence."
let description "Flags there is ... that, it is ... that, and similar openers that delay the real subject."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
allows "Three files hold the config."
allows "There is a lock."
allows "The cache might be stale."
allows "It is advisable that you pin the version."
